<description>
The mempool fee rate estimator can't always return an estimate:

i)   right after first startup (or after re-syncing through IBD) it hasn't tracked
     enough recent blocks yet;
ii)  the mempool is empty or too sparse to fill the requested block percentile,
     e.g. after a cold restart with persistence off or a missing/corrupt
     mempool.dat.
iii) recent blocks cover too little of the mempool (low coverage); and
iv)  the mempool is still loading.

Previously, case (ii) with a healthy mempool will returned the fee rate
floor (the higher of the minimum relay fee and the current mempool minimum fee).
This can be a significant underestimation, since the now-missing mempool may have
held much higher-feerate transactions. The remaining cases surfaced the mempool
error directly from the combined estimate.

This PR makes the combined estimate (`estimatesmartfee` with
`fee_rate_estimator=none`, the default) fall back to the block policy estimate
whenever the mempool estimator isn't ready, instead of returning the floor or
surfacing the error:

- The mempool estimator now returns an error instead of flooring an unfilled
  percentile, so an empty/sparse mempool is treated like any other not-ready
  state rather than potentially silently underestimating.
- The combined estimate starts from the block policy estimate and only lowers it
  with the mempool estimate when one is available. An error is returned only when
  the block policy estimate itself is unavailable.
- Calling `estimatesmartfee` with `fee_rate_estimator=mempool_policy` explicitly
  still returns the failure.

When the mempool fails to load at startup (persistence off, or a missing/corrupt
mempool.dat), init notifies the estimator, which clears its tracked mined-block
window. Otherwise the estimator would keep a window describing a mempool the node
no longer has; clearing it makes the estimator report insufficient data until the
window refills from the current tip, so the combined estimate falls back to block
policy meanwhile.

This is an alternative to #36095.
</description>

<commits>
7b07d5132b fees: fall back to block_policy when the mempool estimator can't estimate

When the mempool policy estimator cannot produce an estimate, GetFeeRateEstimate()
now returns the block policy estimate rather than an error. The combined estimate
starts from the block policy estimate and lowers it with the mempool estimate only
when present, so the fallback path shares the log that reports the selected fee
rate. An error is returned only when the block policy estimate itself is unavailable.

97cffa7212 fees: clear mined-block stats when the mempool load fails

When the mempool fails to load at startup (persistence disabled, or a
missing or corrupt mempool.dat), init notifies the fee rate estimator,
which clears its tracked mined-block window. Otherwise the estimator
would keep a window describing a mempool the node no longer has and,
once the mempool refilled, serve an estimate built on it. Clearing makes
the estimator report insufficient data until the window refills from the
current tip, so the combined estimate falls back to the block policy
estimate meanwhile.

247d47017f fees: cache block-template percentiles instead of floored fee rates

Hoist the Percentiles struct to file scope and cache the raw p50/p75
percentiles, applying the relay floor on each call rather than caching the
already-floored conservative/economical fee rates. The template build moves
into GetOrBuildPercentiles().

Flooring per call lets a later commit tell an unfilled percentile apart from a
floored one, which the warmup fallback needs.

86ef5cc277 fees: return an error instead of a floored mempool fee rate estimate

When the block template cannot fill the requested percentile of a block,
EstimateFeeRate() returns an error rather than flooring the estimate to
the higher of the minimum relay fee and the mempool minimum fee, so the
combined estimator falls back to the block policy estimate.
</commits>

